rial Center","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":20.901321,"lo":-156.482685,"d":1444,"ds":"The Nisei Veterans Memorial Center (Japanese: 二世退役軍人記念センター, Nisei Taiekigunjin Kinen Sentā) is a non-profit organization, memorial, and community center, dedicated to Japanese American nisei veterans. It is located on Kahului, Hawaii and features educational exhibits, a preschool, and an adult daycare. The main building was built in 2006.","wpu":"Nisei Veterans Memorial Center","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Nisei_Veterans_Memorial_Center.jpg/960px-Nisei_Veterans_Memorial_Center.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Halekiʻi-Pihana Heiau State Monument","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":20.908333,"lo":-156.495,"d":2936,"ds":"Halekiʻi-Pihana Heiau State Monument is a 10-acre (4.0 ha) park containing two important luakini heiau on a high ridge near the mouth of ʻĪao Stream in Wailuku, Maui. Both Halekiʻi and Pihana were associated with important Hawaiian chiefs, have been closely studied by archaeologists, and overlook the fertile Nā Wai ʻEhā ('Four Waters') region irrigated by the Wailuku, Waikapu, Waiheʻe and Waiehu streams.","wpu":"Halekiʻi-Pihana Heiau State Monument","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Haleki%27i-Pihana_Heiau_State_Monument.JPG/960px-Haleki%27i-Pihana_Heiau_State_Monument.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kaahumanu Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":20.896944,"lo":-156.500278,"d":3147,"ds":"Kaʻahumanu Church is a church in Wailuku, Maui, Hawaii. The hymns and invocation in the services are in the Hawaiian language. which echo the legacy of Hawaiian churches in the survival of the Hawaiian language where it was banned from being spoken in public.","wpu":"Kaahumanu Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Ka%27ahumanu_Church.JPG/960px-Ka%27ahumanu_Church.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"ʻIao Theater","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":20.88899,"lo":-156.502021,"d":3416,"ds":"The ʻIao Theater is a Spanish Mission style theater opened in 1928, in the city of Wailuku, Maui, Hawaii. It was originally both a movie and vaudeville house, until it fell into disrepair in the 1980s. Facing possible demolition, in 1994, it was listed on the State of Hawaii's Register of Historic Places.","wpu":"ʻIao Theater","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/IaoTheaterFront2010.JPG/960px-IaoTheaterFront2010.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Maui Academy of Performing Arts","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":20.888541,"lo":-156.503547,"d":3582,"ds":"The Maui Academy of Performing Arts (MAPA) is a nonprofit theatre company that produces community theater and offers classes to children and young adults. Over the years, the mainstay of the Academy has been their drama, dance, and summer programs. MAPA was founded in 1974 as the Maui Youth Theater by Linda Takita (daughter of Frankie Yankovic).","wpu":"Maui Academy of Performing Arts","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Location_of_Maui_Academy_of_Performing_Arts.jpg/960px-Location_of_Maui_Academy_of_Performing_Arts.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Spreckelsville, Hawaii","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":20.913503,"lo":-156.434778,"d":4144,"ds":"Spreckelsville is an unincorporated community on the northeastern coast of the west side of the island of Maui in the U.S. state of Hawaii.","wpu":"Spreckelsville, Hawaii","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Spreckelsville_Maui_Hawaii_by_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg/960px-Spreckelsville_Maui_Hawaii_by_Don_Ramey_Logan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Tropical Gardens of Maui","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":20.88298,"lo":-156.51756,"d":5148,"ds":"Tropical Gardens of Maui was a 4-acre (1.6 ha) commercial nursery with botanical garden, located at 200 Iao Valley Road, Wailuku, Maui, Hawaii. The gardens were open daily without charge. The gardens were established in 1987, and are located in a valley floor at approximately 800 feet (240 m) elevation.","wpu":"Tropical Gardens of Maui","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ee/Tropical_Gardens_of_Maui.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Waihee Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":20.935,"lo":-156.515,"d":6375,"ds":"Waihee Church (also known as Waihee Protestant Church) is a historic church in Waihee, Hawaii, United States. The church began construction in 1848 and was finished ten years later in 1858. It is located on a small parcel heavily planted with trees and flowers in the plantation town of Waihee.","wpu":"Waihee Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Maui-Waihee-Protestant-Church-end.JPG/960px-Maui-Waihee-Protestant-Church-end.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Kahului (Hawaiian pronunciation: [kəhuˈluwi]) is an unincorporated community and a census-designated place in Maui County in the U.S. state of Hawaii. It hosts the county's main airport (Kahului Airport), a deep-draft harbor, light industrial areas, and commercial shopping centers. The population was 28,219 at the 2020 census.","wpu":"Kahului, Hawaii","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/AirViewKahului.jpg/960px-AirViewKahului.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}